00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.200 align:start position:0%
the world's religions are beginning to  develop an ecological theology to help  solve the global environmental crisis  Mary Evelyn Tucker and John Grimm  founders of the forum on religion and  ecology at Yale say the world is  witnessing the emergence of a new  religious consciousness that considers  the natural world sacred in probably the  most profound way the religious  traditions for millennia have offered to  humans away a sustaining life in the  midst of tremendous suffering of despair  of a sense of tragedy of facing death  and for some people they would say we  are facing the death of life we are  facing the extinction of species at  twenty and thirty thousand species a  year this has never happened for 65  million years when the dinosaurs were  extinct by an asteroid made extinct so  what does this mean for humans to draw  in a sense that we are altering the life  systems of the planet the air the water  the soils the prospect for food to feed  this number of people that we've  exploded to over six billion people hope  is I think at this moment eluding us in  many ways and yet I do think that if we  can generate the possibilities that  humans have created civilization and  culture over centuries and at this  moment the most crucial thing is that we  can create a sustainable multiform  multi-religious planetary civilization  that says this is one planet we are one  species and we have got to find our role  as humans amidst a larger Earth  community that that's the hope and  actually I think  the excitement about this is that  there's a great work to be done there's  all kinds of work within economics and  technology and politics to make this  actually happen and that's the  empowerment for humans who fundamentally  want to make a difference for the future  in the their deepest aspirations for  their children for their children's  children people are willing to make  sacrifices if they're asked for the  right reasons and that I think tension  between hope and sacrifice and  possibility for a flourishing future is  what religious sensibilities can offer  professors Tucker and grim view this new  relationship between religion and  environment as a broad  interdenominational movement not the  province of any one religion something  new is happening it's like a real of  human earth relations I think that's  part of the passion that that moves us  rather than simply one denomination it's  in all of these denominations like  Vatican to this moment in the Catholic  tradition in the 60s where this would  there was this incredible turn to a  state that the Catholic tradition  recognized other religions and they used  the phrase rays of light that they're a  rays of light and other traditions and  our teacher Thomas berry would step back  and pause for a moment he'd say my  goodness rays of light there are floods  of light in these tradition and so what  we're seeing is we undertake this  project is the the floods of  understanding in these traditions the  radiance of these traditions in their  possibility for guiding people into  renewed human earth reflection all of  the religions have begun to develop  statements on the environment they're up  on the website that Yale has now on the  religion and ecology each of the  traditions has worked through their  processes of their own bureaucracy to  say creation is important in fact sacred  and there's issues here of justice for  example  how the poor are going to be affected by  climate change and so on so there are  general statements on the environment  and now we're finding even more specific  programs and campaigns about issues like  the climate change and global warming  for example there's a very large project  called interface power and light based  in California but involving now over  twenty two states and four thousand  projects of churches and synagogues  involved with examining their energy use  so it's a response to climate change and  changing light bulbs and so on but  raising consciousness it as well it's  been extremely effective the  evangelicals have gotten on board for  climate change in a major way and  they've begun to partner with scientists  this would have been unheard of even  seven years ago and that happened  because two scientists in Oxford  explained to a group of American  evangelicals just about 25 and the facts  of climate change they heard it from  evangelical scientists very high level  people and they said this is an issue of  justice because the poor are going to be  affected all over the world they already  are in Bangladesh and look what's  happened in in Burma  so the evangelicals feel climate change  is very much about God's creation but  also about the poor and the affect on  the poor for climate change religions  long preoccupied with personal salvation  re-examining the role of humans in the  natural world one of the central  challenges for religions as they move  into their ecological phase is to  reinvent their earth orientation and  many of the traditions do have an  otherworldly a personal salvation and  obsession with the interior life only of  the human but always balancing that is a  sense in these traditions that humans  are part of a community whether it's  churches or synagogues or mosques and  they are part of a natural system you  can't have these rituals that were based  often on agricultural cycles the harvest  and the solstice and equinox and  all of these rituals are around the  seasons of the day the seasons of the  year traditions have this in their past  and the question is how they bring it  forward I think the world's religions  are beginning to get on the bus to  figure out how to drive it to look at  the window and respond to what  scientists have been saying about  climate change and pollution and the  whole range of issues and just say yes  we have an issue here that we need to  respond to and one of our approaches is  that the religions be the religious  leaders or laypeople they've got to see  themselves as partners with this  tremendous movement that's decades old  already I mean the first environmental  day was in the early 70s so religions  are late but necessary they are not  sufficient but they complete something  and that is this sensibility that humans  come out of natural processes in an  evolutionary sense and we're deeply part  of bio regional systems we depend on air  on water on this interaction of life of  plant life of animal life and so on and  so religions in their cosmologies their  stories of the origin of the universe  and how humans fit into natural systems  are beginning to re and this is a new  moment for examining their stories and  the role of humans in the natural world  all the rituals of world's religions are  very much nature based you can't have a  Eucharist for example in the Christian  tradition without a sense of wheat of  the earth that brings the wheat forward  or the the grapes for the wine and if  the whole system is polluted what does  this mean for a sense of Eucharist a  sense of Thanksgiving what is that bread  actually imply so a new understanding of  sacrament of ritual is emerging in these  traditions because fundamentally these  traditions take nature as sake  nature is a manifestation of creation of  a created order and whether they have  God involved or not the Western  religions do have a sense that this is a  sacred and created order and that were  stewards were trustees of this created  order professors Tucker and Grimm say  that religions are rediscovering their  ancient traditions in response to the  environmental crisis the joint degree  program here at Yale between the school  of Forestry and environmental studies  and the Divinity School is an academic  approach to the field of religion and  ecology and that means it's  interdenominational it's inter-religious  it's not emphasizing one tradition over  another it's trying to draw on all of  the traditions without idealizing Asian  religions or any any one particular  tradition and that's the excitement that  students can see this objective and yet  engaged study of this conjunction of  religion etiology clearly we're saying  these traditions in their ancient texts  and their scriptures and so on need to  be reconfigured they're not the answers  without rethinking where we are because  they've got to respond to very different  problems than were around when those  scriptures were written but the students  understand as well that there's an  engagement here for transformation of  persons and communities around the world  there's a real possibility in fact even  hope the humanities and the sciences  find common ground in Yale's religion  and ecology program another dimension of  this program that has real traction is  it's a setting in which humanities and  the science are finding a ground for  common dialogue and this is a new and  innovative field where the question of  the humanities and environmental concern  has been unclear and now this area of  values that Mary Ellen has stressed it's  finding a place for investigation in  this type of program but especially the  role of the humanities in this  relationship or what we might say  religions retreated or  the area of cosmology the whole  understanding of what is the world  character of the world it retreated from  that question over five centuries ago  and what we're seeing now I believe is  the re-entry of religion and humanities  broadly speaking into the question of  cosmology this joint program between the  forestry school and Divinity School is  actually the only one of its kind in  North America and in the world so it's  it's really on the edge of a new field  of study that has got tremendous  interest from the students because many  of them have studied abroad especially  in the forestry school and understand  why Buddhism could actually help in the  protection of trees in Thailand and so  on so in the Divinity School students to  really see that religion in responding  to the world at present has to be  involved in ecological issues so the  dynamics in the classroom are absolutely  fascinating between forestry and  environmental study students and the  divinity students I think what's  happening at Yale is unique this program  is an example of that the depth of  exploration that's being undertaken Yale  between the humanities their Sciences  and the Social Sciences and this joint  degree program brings all of those  disciplines to bear it's really a unique  and wonderful time to be at this setting  for more information on the environment  and religion program at Yale University  visit yale.edu slash religion and  ecology for more information about the  Yale School of Forestry and  environmental studies visit environment  Yale dot edu
 

